# Changing the keyboard shortcuts

All of Read Aloud's shortcuts are set in **`ReadAloud.cs`**. You can edit them in Notepad. Nothing needs compiling by hand, because the app rebuilds itself from this file every time it starts.

> **Only want a different *read* shortcut?** You don't need to touch the code. Right-click the speaker icon in the tray, choose **Change read shortcut**, and pick one from the list.

---

## Quick steps

1. **Quit Read Aloud.** Right-click the speaker icon in the tray and choose **Quit Read Aloud**.
2. **Open the code.** Go to your `Documents\Read Aloud` folder, right-click `ReadAloud.cs`, and choose **Open with → Notepad**.
3. **Find the shortcuts.** Press **Ctrl+F** and search for `shortcuts = new[]`.
4. **Edit the line** for the shortcut you want to change. See the sections below.
5. **Save** with **Ctrl+S**.
6. **Start Read Aloud again** from the Desktop shortcut.
7. **Changed the *read* shortcut (Id 1)?** Also follow [Clear the saved read shortcut](#important-clear-the-saved-read-shortcut), or your change will be ignored.

If you make a typing mistake, Read Aloud shows a **"Read Aloud couldn't start"** message that says which line is wrong. Fix the line, save, and start it again.

> **Tip:** before editing, copy `ReadAloud.cs` somewhere safe so you can always go back.

---

## Where the shortcuts are

Search for `shortcuts = new[]`. You'll find this block:

```csharp
shortcuts = new[] {
    new Shortcut { Id = 1, Mods = MOD_CONTROL | MOD_ALT, Key = Keys.R,  Label = "Read highlighted text / Stop", Run = ReadOrStop },
    new Shortcut { Id = 2, Mods = MOD_CONTROL | MOD_ALT, Key = Keys.P,  Label = "Pause / Resume",               Run = () => synth.PauseOrResume() },
    new Shortcut { Id = 3, Mods = MOD_CONTROL | MOD_ALT, Key = Keys.Oemplus,  Label = "Speak faster",           Run = () => ChangeRate(+1) },
    new Shortcut { Id = 4, Mods = MOD_CONTROL | MOD_ALT, Key = Keys.OemMinus, Label = "Speak slower",           Run = () => ChangeRate(-1) },
    new Shortcut { Id = 5, Mods = MOD_CONTROL | MOD_ALT, Key = Keys.V,  Label = "Next voice",                   Run = NextVoice },
};
```

Each line is one shortcut. Only these two parts decide which keys you press:

| Part | What it means | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `Mods = ...` | The keys you **hold down** | `MOD_CONTROL \| MOD_ALT` = Ctrl+Alt |
| `Key = ...` | The key you **press** while holding them | `Keys.R` = the R key |

Leave `Id`, `Label` and `Run` as they are, unless you're adding a new shortcut (see the end of this guide).

---

## The modifier keys (`Mods`)

Join modifiers with a **`|`** (the vertical bar, Shift+\\ on most keyboards).

| You want to hold | Write |
|---|---|
| Ctrl+Alt | `MOD_CONTROL \| MOD_ALT` |
| Ctrl+Shift | `MOD_CONTROL \| MOD_SHIFT` |
| Alt+Shift | `MOD_ALT \| MOD_SHIFT` |
| Ctrl+Alt+Shift | `MOD_CONTROL \| MOD_ALT \| MOD_SHIFT` |
| Ctrl only | `MOD_CONTROL` |
| Nothing (single key) | `0` |

Use `0` only for keys you never type with, such as **Pause/Break**, **Scroll Lock** or **F13–F24**. A letter key with no modifier would stop that letter working everywhere.

---

## The main key (`Key`)

| Key | Write |
|---|---|
| Letters A–Z | `Keys.A` … `Keys.Z` |
| Number row 0–9 | `Keys.D0` … `Keys.D9` |
| Function keys | `Keys.F1` … `Keys.F24` |
| Number pad | `Keys.NumPad0` … `Keys.NumPad9`, `Keys.Add`, `Keys.Subtract`, `Keys.Multiply`, `Keys.Divide` |
| Space bar | `Keys.Space` |
| Pause/Break | `Keys.Pause` |
| Scroll Lock | `Keys.Scroll` |
| Insert / Home / End | `Keys.Insert`, `Keys.Home`, `Keys.End` |
| Page Up / Page Down | `Keys.PageUp`, `Keys.PageDown` |
| Arrow keys | `Keys.Up`, `Keys.Down`, `Keys.Left`, `Keys.Right` |
| `=` and `-` | `Keys.Oemplus`, `Keys.OemMinus` |
| `,` and `.` | `Keys.Oemcomma`, `Keys.OemPeriod` |
| `/` | `Keys.OemQuestion` |
| `;` | `Keys.Oem1` |

Capitals matter. `Keys.PageUp` works, but `keys.pageup` won't.

